founder of instagram  so that was a big lesson in how the  world works um  i know it was  i was like wow  so  a few months later i left facebook and  then as soon as i left i was like  there's something to this i want to  explore emojis so  i did that luckily at one day brexit  happened i was reading this article  called the world's reaction to brexit in  emoji again i'm like quartz they steal  my idea again everyone's always stealing  my idea  and then  that's a that's a delusion you have when  you're an innovator you think everyone's  stealing your idea when the reality is  no one cares um  and uh  there's uh yeah so i read this article  and i'm like okay what is this article  luckily this is the whole article  there's no data in this article it's  just a silly article like a meme article  like i don't know what to call this it's  it's not a good article but and this was  my reaction to this article in emoji  and then i was like listen i don't have  to just sit here like hate tweet this  article which is really bad i can  actually do the thing they promised they  would do i can look at the emoji  reaction to brexit so at this point i  locked myself in my room for three days  and i engage in a one-man 72-hour  hackathon learning how to download  tweets using the twitter api learning  how to pull emojis from the tweets  learning how to look at the emojis with  how they're used with words i did that  in my room for 72 hours i don't even  know how i did it i can't i can barely  you know read it read a book now  but uh so that's also another lesson for  the younger people you know make the  most of your youth when you have it in  your attention span  um and at the end of those 72 hours i  had an article published in vice called  here are the most popular emojis from  the brexit reaction um i had been  dabbling in journalism for a while i  emailed a friend there six minutes later  she responded from her iphone this is  wonderful we're going to publish this  tomorrow and that's how i became an  emoji data scientist and then you're  wondering what did i do was it some  super complicated you know nlp like not  really i just counted stuff right i  looked at these are the top emojis  people use and tweets about brexit and  you see that you know they're all types  of emojis they're real genuine emotions  and back then a lot of people thought  emojis were silly they thought this is  trivial people are this is noise that's  what a lot of people thought and here  you can see actually people are using  them to express happiness sadness anger  disappointment there's the british flag  there there's a lot of emojis there and  what you also notice that the emojis are  correlated with the hashtags people use  this for me was mind-blowing again  everyone in this room is obvious right  but back then for a lot of people it was  very noteworthy that the british flag  emoji heavily over indexes on people who  use the hashtag vote leave right they're  saying votely we wanted brexit to happen  we're very patriotic the celebratory  party had emoji over indexes on vote  leave these are people happy brexit  happened the crying emoji over indexes  on vote remained they're sad it happened  the praying had emoji vote remain  because initially they thought there  might be a re-vote  and this was essentially my article  right and it kind of went viral all  these people started calling me they're  like emoji data sciences what is that  have you looked into this this and this  and i basically spent maybe a year just  giving talks about this and i was on tv  and it was a really exciting journey um  i did a lot of cool stuff i looked at  the kanye west taylor swift controversy  i don't know if anyone here remembers  that  it was uh this is one of those things  where i feel it's like a niche topic and  then i i was giving a talk at this in  morocco at the at the data science  master's students these kids literally  started jumping up and down when i got  to this they were screaming like they  all had positions they were there was a  justin bieber camp there was a drake  camp like everyone had they all knew  everything they were like well then  justin bieber did this and then they  tweeted this and then taylor had this  lyric like these are people felt very  strongly about this and what i found in  this analysis again very simple i i like  to say a lot of data science and  analytics is just counting things and  labeling them that's a lot because  especially in an area where people  haven't done that before and back then  this was fairly novel um now this has  become a big field emoji nlp i think  there have been 50 phds written on emoji  nlp most of them have cited my work but  again a lot of that is for a very niche  audience right those are computer  scientists trying to prove some new  methodologies in nlp i think the  innovation here was that you can use  this to get people excited about data  science you can use this to get people  excited about r um so here what we  notice is that taylor swift a lot a lot  of people in tweets mention killer use  the snake emoji this was the world's  first instance of snake bombing so  here's another way of representing this  data  where everything on the left are emojis  more often used when people are  mentioning taylor everything on the  right are emojis more often used when  people are mentioning kanye and again  you see that snake showing up there so  taylor swift actually complained to  instagram to kevin she said this is a  cyber bullying and instagram banned the  snake emoji for 72 hours from instagram  you could not comment the snake emoji  because taylor swift was threatening to  go public with a lawsuit saying because  everyone is going to taylor's post snake  snakes thanks thank you that's all  kane's fans were doing and it was wild  and again this was like really cool that  you can actually use um you know you can  emojis are data right and as data  scientists this is something where we  can investigate we can build tools um we  can get people really excited about  about this uh the power we have as data  scientists i also looked at the emoji  language of fan love so this is when dj  khaled was really big  i just looked at a bunch of celebrities  i looked at the top emojis people use  about them very surprising is that four  of the five emojis are the same um it's  the heart eyes emoji it's the heart it's  the tears of joy it's a loudly crying  emoji  for me this was really beautiful because  it's like no one is teaching us how to  use emojis it's really it's chaos right  there's like there used to be two  thousand emojis now there might be four  or five thousand emojis yet  the most common emojis people use to  talk about their favorite fan  celebrities are just these four it's  like somehow there's order emerging from  chaos  and that is you know what we're really  striving to find as data scientists  trying to find those patterns and again  no one is teaching people how to do this  yet somehow we're coalescing around a  common usage of this language which is  which is kind of beautiful  so  let's turn to something a little bit  more serious um election night you know  election night a lot of statisticians  are out there trying to do cool stuff  they're trying to predict they were  looking at who was going to win  i was also looking at data i was looking  at emoji data  so on election night i had a streamer  running i pulled like 20 million tweets  um all throughout election day and i did  something very simple i looked at emojis  coming in after the results started to  be announced around midnight midnight  eastern time and then i looked at all  the emojis used after midnight and i  looked at emoji's jews when people were  talking about trump and emojis people  use when talking about hillary clinton  and again this is one of those charts  that really blew my mind because  it's really powerful right like you see  on the donald trump side you see both  celebratory emojis right the party hat  emoji the clapping emoji you also see  the middle finger emoji which i had  never seen in an analysis before  um  and then and then on the hillary clinton  side you see you know  crying  the heart praying  like a really you know a really powerful  visual portrait of the the state of a  nation almost  and i think this was really exciting um  just being able to find this right it  was out there right everyone has access  most people a lot of people have access  to twitter data but you know it depends  what you go looking for and what you  find  um you know there's a lot of interesting  things in that you know in that data set  you can look at the time course of  emojis used throughout the day and you  see the american flag spikes right after  midnight in tweets mentioning trump you  see the crying emoji spikes and tweets  mentioning hillary clinton starting  around 9 p.m you see the middle finger  is elevated for trump throughout the day  if it comes uh  it becomes especially elevated around  you know 7 or 8 p.m and then you see the  praying hat for hillary spikes 11 p.m  that's when like michigan was announced  pennsylvania was announced people were  literally like oh my god there's one  more state can she win  and it's like so beautiful right like  it's represented in the data like how  how incredible is that i also looked at  the words people use  when they when they use these emojis  that's what the middle finger emoji  means  i'll take my nobel prize now you know  that's my  i know i know everyone's been wondering  what does it mean that's what it means  that's what my analysis that's what all  of my graduate school training and data  science expertise reveals  um and uh you know i think we it's funny  but also i think it's really real right  because again  you look at some of these tweets one of  these is how can i go to work in the  morning knowing the people i work with  voted for trump thinking face em